      "review": {
        "measured_outcome": "The attempted kernel with tile sizes BM=32, BN=32, BK=64 and 2-stage software pipeline showed a significant speedup over the baseline (3.14 TFLOPS vs 1.04 TFLOPS), confirming improved data reuse and overlapping of memory loads and computation. Register usage increased to 127 per thread with 4096 bytes shared memory, no spills, indicating higher resource use but stable execution. The grid size and warp count remained unchanged, saturating GPU reasonably well. Boundary masking is correctly applied to handle non-multiple dimensions.",
        "failures": "Despite improvements, register usage is quite high (127 per thread), which could limit occupancy and thus peak throughput. The kernel relies on direct loads into registers without explicit shared memory buffering for tiles, which might limit operand reuse and cause some memory inefficiency. The grid size (17,49) may not fully saturate all 108 SMs considering high resource usage per block. Masking overhead at boundaries remains but is unavoidable due to input dimensions. The pipeline is conceptually present but implemented as a simple loop without explicit double-buffering or asynchronous loads, possibly limiting overlap.",
        "next_directions": [
          "Incorporate explicit shared memory buffering for tiles of A and B to improve data reuse and reduce global memory traffic, potentially lowering register pressure per thread.",
